.g., a decomposition window, a periodic-prior length, or a reshape axis). All other hyperparameters are held fixed so that **any change in metric is attributable to `period` alone**.\\n\\n### 2.2 Sweep \\u2014 candidate values\\nGiven ETTm1's 15-minute sampling:\\n- **Anchor values justified by data calendar:** 96 (1 day), 288 (3 days), 672 (1 week), 1440 (\\u224810 days), 2880 (\\u224830 days).\\n- **Sub-seasonal / off-cycle values to check robustness:** 24 (6 h), 48 (12 h), 192 (2 days), 2016 (\\u224814 days), 43200 = cap at train length / 2.\\n- **Baseline reference:** the default `period` value used by the chosen reference implementation (must be specified by the researcher \\u2014 see assumptions).\\n\\nA two-stage sweep is suggested: (a) a coarse sweep over all anchors above with 1\\u20132 seeds; (b) a fine sweep around the best anchor(s) with 3+ seeds.\\n\\n### 2.3 Dataset & splits\\n- **Dataset:** ETTm1 (publicly distributed `ETTm1.csv`, columns: date, HUFL, HULL, MUFL, MULL, LUFL, LULL, OT; freq = 15 min).\\n- **Standard split convention** (the de-facto splits used in Autoformer/Informer/PatchTST papers): train 12 months, val 4 months, test 4 months with fixed chronological boundaries, using a rolling-eval protocol (last `pred_len` of each sliding window as the test target).\\n- `train/val/test` windows are constructed **once and shared** across every period value to isolate the effect of `period`.\\n\\n### 2.4 Fixed evaluation contract (identical for every run)\\n- **Look-back / input length `seq_len`:** fixed. Common choice in the literature: 96, with 336 as a secondary length. (To be confirmed.)\\n- **Forecast horizons `pred_len`:** fixed at the standard ETTm1 suite, e.g. **96, 192, 336, 720**.\\n- **Channels:** all 6 (HUFL, HULL, MUFL, MULL, LUFL, LULL) + OT, multivariate evaluation; univariate on OT reported as a secondary view.\\n- **Normalization:** StandardScaler fit on train slice only, applied identically to val/test.\\n- **Metrics:** **MSE** and **MAE**, computed on de-normalized test predictions, reported per horizon, with **mean \\u00b1 std across seeds**.\\n- **Seeds:** 3 seeds for fine sweep, 1 seed for coarse sweep; identical seed list across every `period` value. Library-level seeds set for python, numpy, torch (CPU), torch.backends.cudnn.\\n\\n### 2.5 Controls (to isolate the effect of period)\\n| Item | Treatment | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Preprocessing | identical CSV \\u2192 windows | removes data-drift |\\n| Splits | fixed train/val/test boundaries | removes split-noise |\\n| Label scaler | fit once on train, reused | removes normalization bias |\\n| All non-period HP | identical across runs | isolates the period effect |\\n| Epoch budget | fixed, identical early-stopping rule on val | removes training-time drift |\\n| Optimizer, LR, batch size, dropout | fixed | removes optimisation confound |\\n| Selection rule | pick epoch with best val MSE **per (period, seed) cell**, then evaluate on test | standard protocol; no test-set tuning |\\n\\n### 2.6 \\\"Direct comparison\\\" guarantee\\nFor every `(period, horizon, seed)` cell, a single deterministic run is produced. To compare two periods, we use **paired differences** within each seed before averaging, removing between-seed variance and giving a tighter sensitivity read on `period`.\\n\\n### 2.7 Deliverable format (design \\u2014 no execution)\\n- A single result table with rows = `period` \\u00d7 `pred_len` \\u00d7 `seed`, columns = test MSE, test MAE.\\n- A second summary table with mean \\u00b1 std across seeds per cell.\\n- A short ablation: best vs default period on each horizon.\\n- Optional diagnostic: learning curves and per-channel residuals, only if the researcher requests them later.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. ASSUMPTIONS & UNCERTAINTIES\\n\\n### Assumed (taken because the workspace provides nothing)\\n- **(A1) Definition of the \\\"PDF model.\\\"** [assumed]\\n  Working definition: a neural forecaster that takes an integer `period` hyperparameter governing how the sequence is decomposed, folded, or matched against a periodic prior. Without a reference implementation in the workspace, this is the general class of \\\"seasonality-aware\\\" forecasters (closest analogues: DLinear-style moving-average decomposition with window length, N-BEATS block-period, TimesNet's FFT-derived period). The intended specific model is **unknown** and should be clarified.\\n\\n- **(A2) Meaning of \\\"period.\\\"** [assumed]\\n  A single integer representing the seasonal / cycle length in number of time steps (steps, not minutes). Plausible candidate meanings to disambiguate: (i) decomposition window, (ii) kernel length, (iii) reshape-grid dimension, (iv) PatchTST-style patch length. Researcher clarification required for which axis the knob acts on.\\n\\n- **(A3) ETTm1 split convention.** [assumed]\\n  The standard 12/4/4-month chronological split used by Autoformer/Info*n*-former/PatchTST literature. I do **not** assume the bespoke 6/2/2 split sometimes seen in ablation papers unless confirmed.\\n\\n- **(A4) Baseline (`reference`) period.** [assumed]\\n  The baseline period that the \\\"different period settings\\\" are being compared against is the implementation default; its value is unknown. I have proposed anchor values that are natural for ETTm1's calendar as defaults if no other is given.\\n\\n- **(A5) Look-back and horizon suite.** [assumed]\\n  `seq_len \\u2208 {96}` and `pred_len \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}`. These are the standard ETTm1 horizons and should be confirmed.\\n\\n- **(A6) Reproducibility environment.** [assumed]\\n  CPU-only, Python 3.12, PyTorch installed at runtime (not preinstalled), 24 threads, no GPU.\\n\\n### Unknown / to-be-clarified\\n- **(U1) Which \\\"PDF model\\\" reference is intended** \\u2014 no PDF-named forecasting model with a \\\"period\\\" hyperparameter is present in the workspace or visible in the standard time-series-forecasting codebases that I normally read. Possibilities the researcher should rule in/out:\\n  - a specific recent paper's acronym (e.g., \\\"Period-\\u2026 Decomposition \\u2026 Function/Forecasting/Filtering\\\");\\n  - a vendor or internal model;\\n  - a notational misnomer for \\\"PDF forecasting\\\" in the sense of probabilistic density forecasting (in which \\\"period\\\" would not normally be a primary knob).\\n- **(U2) The data loader / CSV path / normalization pipeline** \\u2014 to be specified.\\n- **(U3) Training budget** \\u2014 max epochs, early-stopping patience, learning rate schedule.\\n- **(U4) Whether ETTm1 evaluation is univariate (OT only) or multivariate (7 channels)** \\u2014 the two are not interchangeable.\\n- **(U5) Whether the period knob is consumed only at the input-reshape stage, or also inside loss / regulariser** \\u2014 affects which periods are even legal.\\n- **(U6) Whether the model has any other period-adjacent knobs** (e.g., multiple periods, learnable period, period dropout) that would need to be held constant concurrently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### What I am NOT doing in this turn\\n- I am **not** running training or downloading the PDF model, the ETTm1 dataset, or any external dependency.\\n- I am **not** inventing results, table numbers, or \\\"expected\\\" performance figures.\\n- I am **not** selecting a specific open-source reference repo for the \\\"PDF model\\\" \\u2014 workspace is silent and `track.md` confirms it is not required for design.\\n- I am awaiting explicit researcher approval (or clarification on U1\\u2013U6) before moving to `stage=result` with the full experimental design document.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"\\nEvidence underpinning the inspection:\\n- `ls -la /workspace` -> single 389-byte file `/workspace/task.json`; no other contents.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` -> confirms design-only task; cites `track.md` which is itself absent (\\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\").\\n- `ls -la /home/actor`, `ls -la /tmp` -> both empty.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'ettm1*'` -> no matches.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'patchtst*' / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'dlinear*' / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'timesnet*'` -> no matches.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*.pdf'` -> only CUPS printer test pages under `/usr/share/cups/data/` (system, not research PDFs).\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*pdf*'` -> only `libqpdf.so` and CLI helpers (`pdf2ps`, `pdftotext`, ...); no forecasting model.\\n- `python3 --version` -> 3.12.3; `pip3 list` shows only base system packages (no torch/numpy/pandas visible in the head-of-list); `nvidia-smi` -> driver not available; `nproc` -> 24.\\n\\nThe question, dataset name (ETTm1), and the ETTm1 calendar facts (15-min sampling \\u2192 96 steps/day, 672 steps/week) are public reference knowledge, not sourced from the workspace.\\n\", \"assumptions\": [\"(A1, assumed) \\\"PDF model\\\" = a seasonality-aware neural forecaster with an explicit integer period hyperparameter; the exact reference implementation is unknown because no relevant artefact exists in the workspace.</input>\\n<parameter name=\\\"data\\\">[(A1, assumed) \\\"PDF model\\\" = a seasonality-aware neural forecaster with an explicit integer period hyperparameter; the exact reference implementation is unknown because no relevant artefact exists in the workspace.\\n(A2, assumed) \\\"period\\\" = an integer number of time steps (seasonal/cycle length) consumed by the model; precise role (decomposition window, reshape axis, kernel length, patch length) is unknown.\\n(A3, assumed) ETTm1 splits = standard 12/4/4-month chronological split used by the Autoformer / Informer / PatchTST family of papers.\\n(A4, assumed) Baseline period = the implementation's default value; its numeric default is unknown.\\n(A5, assumed) Fixed evaluation contract: seq_len in {96}, pred_len in {96, 192, 336, 720}, channels = multivariate (7) and OT-only univariate as secondary, MSE + MAE on de-normalized targets.\\n(A6, assumed) Reproducibility: CPU-only Python 3.12, 24 threads, no GPU; PyTorch and data dependencies to be installed at runtime.\\n\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"(U1) Which specific \\\"PDF model\\\" reference is intended \\u2014 the workspace contains no source, and no widely-cited forecasting model by that acronym with a \\\"period\\\" knob is identifiable from within-workspace evidence.</parameter>\\n<parameter name=\\\"data\\\">[(U1) Which specific \\\"PDF model\\\" reference is intended \\u2014 the workspace contains no source, and no widely-cited forecasting model by that acronym with a \\\"period\\\" knob is identifiable from within-workspace evidence.\\n(U2) Data loader path, raw CSV location, and normalization pipeline specification.\\n(U3) Training budget (max epochs, early-stopping patience, LR schedule) and optimization hyperparameters being held fixed.\\n(U4) Univariate (OT only) vs multivariate (7 channels) evaluation target for ETTm1.\\n(U5) Whether the period knob touches only the input reshape, or also internal loss/regularizer terms \\u2014 affects which period values are legal.\\n(U6) Whether the model has additional period-adjacent knobs (e.g. multiple periods, learnable period, period dropout) that need to be pinned simultaneously.\\n\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-2972c3fd-72e1-4161-a5f1-45ddc9e4a72a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
