{"id": "980f87a4-1bcc-42da-8074-cda91a6aadc7", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:43:03.651606", "source": "user", "parent_id": "c690663e-236f-4529-9edf-6822f0ec7534", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with working assumptions locked in. The plan is feasible as a design-only deliverable: (1) the workspace genuinely contains no PDF-model source or ETTm1 data, so any concrete period-interpretation must be assumption-based, and the agent has narrowed this to a single clear working definition (period = integer cycle length in time steps consumed by a seasonality-aware forecaster); (2) the inbuilt controls are appropriate for isolating the period effect (fixed splits, fixed scaler, fixed non-period hyperparameters, identical seeds, paired-difference comparison); (3) the calendar-anchored sweep (96/288/672/1440/2880 with off-cycle robustness values) gives interpretable coverage without exploding the design. Proceed to produce the final experiment-design document. The deliverable must be the design itself — no execution, no fabricated metrics, no invented expected outcomes. Required structure: (A) Research question and hypotheses corresponding to period-setting effects; (B) Operational definition of the period knob with explicit note that the specific PDF-model implementation is unknown and design is implementation-agnostic; (C) Period values to be swept (anchors + off-cycle) with rationale per value; (D) Dataset & fixed evaluation contract (ETTm1, 12/4/4 chronological split, seq_len, pred_len suite, multivariate + OT-only univariate channels, MSE/MAE on de-normalized targets, seeds protocol); (E) Controls table as proposed; (F) Analysis plan including paired-difference comparison, per-horizon breakdown, mean±std aggregation, no-test-set tuning; (G) Feasibility & runtime estimate narrative for CPU-only 24-thread environment with explicit acknowledgement that no GPU and no preinstalled PyTorch/data deps exist; (H) Explicit limitations: design is implementation-agnostic and reservations tied to unknowns U1–U6; (I) Open questions retained for the researcher. Keep it self-contained — no fabricated numbers, no claims of execution.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "e2296984-b18d-4c1f-8653-b3005cc737bf", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:43:59.035555", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "980f87a4-1bcc-42da-8074-cda91a6aadc7", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "\n# Experiment Design: Effect of the `period` setting on the PDF model — ETTm1\n\n> **Status:** Design only. Not executed. No result numbers invented. No reference repo adopted. Where the source material is silent, items are flagged **assumed** vs **unknown** using the prefixes **(Ax)** / **(Ux)** from the approved plan.\n\n---\n\n## (A) Research Question and Pre-registered Hypotheses\n\n**RQ.** *How do different settings of the `period` hyperparameter of the PDF model affect its forecasting performance on the ETTm1 dataset under a fixed evaluation contract?*\n\nThe question is decomposed into four pre-registered claims, all stated in a **two-sided** form so that any observed direction is informative.\n\n- **H1 (coarse shape).** Test MSE/MAE vary non-monotonically with `period` across at least two calendar anchors of ETTm1 (daily `96`, weekly `672`).\n  - *Why it matters.* If the surface were monotonic, then a single extreme value would dominate and the period knob would be effectively redundant given a long enough look-back. Non-monotonicity is the signal that the knob carries real information about cycle-length alignment.\n- **H2 (alignment prior).** Calendar-anchored periods (multiples of 96 steps, the native daily cycle of ETTm1's 15-minute grid) yield lower test MSE/MAE than the immediately adjacent off-cycle values, on average across the horizon suite.\n  - *Why it matters.* Separates \"period matters\" from \"period matters only when its value aligns with the data calendar.\"\n- **H3 (horizon dependence).** The magnitude of any `period` effect grows with the forecast horizon `pred_len`, holding look-back fixed.\n  - *Why it matters.* A purely short-horizon effect (≤96 steps) would invalidate the knob for long-range forecasting on ETTm1.\n- **H4 (off-cycle sensitivity).** Off-cycle values (e.g., `48`, `288`, `1440`, `2016`) still produce comparable or improved performance within a paired-seed difference of at most 10 % MSE on at least one horizon, when measured against the best calendar anchor.\n  - *Why it matters.* Tests whether the knob is fragile to small mismatches or whether it generalises.\n\n**Pre-registration rule.** H1–H4 are evaluated only on **de-normalized test targets** under the fixed evaluation contract in §D. The test split is *never* used for any selection; only val is.\n\n---\n\n## (B) Operational Definition of the `period` Knob\n\nThe workspace contains **no PDF-model source, no ETTm1 data, and no prior experiment script** (see inspection in the plan stage). The design is therefore **implementation-agnostic** and pins only what travels with the term itself.\n\n**(A2, assumed)** `period ∈ ℕ` is a single positive integer denoting a **cycle length in number of time steps** at the model's native sampling frequency. The knob is consumed by whatever stage of the PDF model would in general consume a seasonality length — for implementation families that span decomposers (e.g., moving-average-based linear forecasters), reshape-based encoders, or period-matching decoders, this is the single scalar that the model exposes as \"the period.\" Whether the period also appears in auxiliary places (positional bias, regulariser, multi-period head) is **unknown (U5)** and is investigated via ablations in §G.\n\n**Specification language.** For every run we record, *as a metadata field but never as a decision input*:\n\n| Metadata field | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `model_class` | string identifier of the PDF implementation used |\n| `period` | the integer value under test |\n| `period_consumers` | which callable modules read `period` (e.g., decomposition window, reshape grid) — if unknown, log `unknown` |\n| `period_dependent_init` | are any weight initialisations conditioned on `period`? — flag if yes |\n\nThis metadata is exactly what lets the conclusions remain valid across implementations when U1 is later resolved.\n\n**Working vocabulary used throughout this document:**\n\n- \"Calendar anchor\" = a `period` that is an integer multiple of 96 (the daily cycle of ETTm1 at 15-minute resolution).\n- \"Off-cycle value\" = a `period` that is *not* an integer multiple of 96.\n- \"Period cell\" = the tuple `(period, pred_len, seed)` that produces a single evaluated run.\n\n---\n\n## (C) Sweep — Period Values and Rationale Per Value\n\n### C.1 Coarse sweep (1 seed per cell, all horizons)\nCalendar anchors — chosen because they are the empirically plausible seasonal cycles at 15-minute resolution:\n\n| `period` | Rationale |\n|---|---|\n| **96** | 1 day. ETTm1 is electricity-transformer load with a clear 24-h cycle; this is the **shortest natural anchor** and the most commonly used default in linear-decomposition forecasters. |\n| **288** | 3 days. Tests sensitivity to a sub-weekly but multi-daily choice. |\n| **672** | 1 week (96 × 7). ETTm1 exhibits weekly structure (weekday/weekend load shift); this is the **most data-aligned weekly anchor**. |\n| **1440** | 10 days. Off-week but still on the day grid; controls for weekly dominance. |\n| **2880** | ~30 days. A monthly anchor; tests long-period extrapolation given fixed look-back. |\n\nOff-cycle robustness values — chosen because they do **not** land on a calendar multiple:\n\n| `period` | Rationale |\n|---|---|\n| **24** | 6 hours. Far shorter than a day; tests the lower end of the regime. |\n| **48** | 12 hours. Off-grid; tests whether \"near-daily\" but unaligned hurts. |\n| **192** | 2 days. Day-grid but skips the canonical 1-day value. |\n| **2016** | ~14 days. Off-grid between weekly and bi-weekly. |\n| **4320** | cap = floor(train_len / 2) under the 12/4/4 split. Stress-test of \"very long period\". |\n\n**Reference baseline** **(A4, assumed)**: the implementation's default `period`. Numeric value **unknown (U4)**, to be clipped into the sweep lattice at logging time.\n\n### C.2 Fine sweep (3 seeds per cell)\nConducted only around the two cells that win the coarse sweep on validation-MSE per horizon:\n\n- A neighbourhood of width ±1 step at the winning off-cycle value.\n- A neighbourhood of width ±24 steps at the winning calendar anchor.\n- The implementation default (if not already in the coarse sweep) is added with 3 seeds.\n\nThis is the **only** step that uses more than one seed, in line with the approved plan.\n\n**Sweep cell budget (upper bound, used only for feasibility planning in §G):**\n\n| Stage | `period` values | horizons | seeds | cells |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coarse | 11 (5 anchors + 5 off + default) | 4 | 1 | ≤ 44 |\n| Fine | ≤ 6 (the two winners ± their neighbourhoods + default) | 4 | 3 | ≤ 72 |\n| Total | — | — | — | **≤ 116 period-cells** |\n\n---\n\n## (D) Dataset and Fixed Evaluation Contract\n\n### D.1 Dataset\n- **Name:** ETTm1. Publicly distributed as `ETTm1.csv` with column `date` + 6 oil-temperature features (`HUFL`, `HULL`, `MUFL`, `MULL`, `LUFL`, `LULL`) + the target `OT`. **(Ax, assumed)** — file is not present in the workspace and must be obtained at runtime per environment note.\n\n### D.2 Splits (identical across every period cell)\n- **Chronological 12 / 4 / 4 months**, the standard split used by Autoformer / Informer / PatchTST literature on ETTm1.\n- Train slice = first 12 months; val = next 4 months; test = final 4 months.\n- Sliding windows for evaluation: for each `(seq_len, pred_len)`, evaluate on every contiguous window whose *prediction endpoint* lies in the test slice, **and only in the test slice**; this is the rolling \"last-window\" protocol.\n- Splits are **materialised once** at the start of the experiment and reused; a hash of `(timestamps, scaler_state)` is logged so reproducibility can be checked.\n\n### D.3 Normalization\n- `StandardScaler` fit on the **train slice only** (mean and std of each channel). Applied identically to train, val, and test. The scaler state is serialised.\n- Metrics computed on **de-normalized** predictions and targets. **(Ax, assumed)**\n\n### D.4 Fixed evaluation contract (every cell must obey)\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `seq_len` (look-back) | **96** (with **336** as mandatory secondary length if runner budget allows; logged but not used for any decision) |\n| `pred_len` (horizon suite) | **{96, 192, 336, 720}** |\n| Channels, multivariate | all 7 (`HUFL`–`OT`) used as input and forecast jointly |\n| Channels, univariate (secondary) | `OT` only, reported separately |\n| Loss during training | MSE on (possibly normalised) targets, model-specific |\n| Reporting metrics | **MSE** and **MAE**, on de-normalized test targets |\n| Aggregation across seeds | **mean ± std** |\n| Selection variable | **val MSE**, per `(period, pred_len, seed)` cell |\n| Test-set usage | **none** for selection, thresholding, or hyper-tuning |\n\n### D.5 Seeds protocol\n- Fixed cross-cell seed list: `S = {s1, s2, s3}` for fine cells; `S = {s1}` for coarse cells.\n- Each seed initialises: Python `random`, NumPy, PyTorch (CPU), and `torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True`.\n- Data-loader worker seeds = seed + worker_id (modulo 1e9).\n- A single seed run is **fully reproducible** under these settings on the same pinned dependency versions.\n\n---\n\n## (E) Controls (to isolate the effect of `period`)\n\nThe intent is that **any metric difference between two `period` cells of equal seed, horizon, and look-back is attributable solely to `period`**, up to the residual noise acknowledged in (F).\n\n| Item | Treatment | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw data | single CSV, single SHA256 | eliminates data-drift |\n| Splits | materialised once, hashed | eliminates split-noise |\n| Scaler | fit once on train, reused | eliminates normalization bias |\n| `seq_len` | fixed across the design (with secondary length logged) | isolates the period axis |\n| `pred_len` | fixed per cell | equal-horizon comparability |\n| Non-period hyperparameters (e.g., hidden size, depth, LR, batch size, optimizer, dropout, weight decay, gradient clipping) | **identical** across cells | removes optimisation confound |\n| Training budget | fixed max-epoch budget + identical early-stopping rule on val MSE per cell | removes training-time drift |\n| Multi-period / learnable-period / period-dropout knobs **(U6)** | **pinned** to a constant value or disabled; if disabling breaks the model, that is documented and the cell is excluded | eliminates auxiliary knobs |\n| Hardware and software | pinned versions; CPU-only | removes hardware variance |\n| Seeds | cross-cell identical seed list | enables paired-difference in §F |\n| Selection variable | val MSE only | no test-set tuning |\n\nIf any control above must be violated because the implementation requires it (e.g., the period knob forces a specific input shape), the violation is logged as `control_violation` metadata and the affected cells are not used for cross-implementation claims.\n\n---\n\n## (F) Analysis Plan\n\n### F.1 Aggregation\n- For each `(period, pred_len, channel_set)` cell with multiple seeds, compute mean and std of test MSE and test MAE across seeds.\n- A grand-mean table over `pred_len` for each `period` is reported as a secondary summary.\n\n### F.2 Paired-difference comparison (the primary signal)\n- For any two period values `p` and `q`, form `Δ_i = metric(p, seed_i) − metric(q, seed_i)` within the **same seed `i`** at the **same horizon and the same channel set**.\n- Hypothesis tests:\n  - **Wilcoxon signed-rank** across seed pairs (robust to non-Gaussianity, 3-seed-friendly).\n  - Sign-test over seeds as a non-parametric fallback if n=3 is too small for Wilcoxon power (n=3 yields no p-value; only direction is reported).\n- Effect size: **median paired difference** and **relative paired difference** (`median(Δ) / median(metric(q))`).\n- A confidence band is **not** reported at n=3; the design specifies \"n seeds for variance read\", not \"power analysis\", per the approved plan.\n\n### F.3 Per-horizon breakdown (H3)\n- Estimate the effect size `|median(Δ_bad − Δ_good)|` per horizon and report its sign. The H3 claim is supported if the magnitude grows monotonically (or at least non-trivially) from `pred_len=96` to `pred_len=720`.\n\n### F.4 Off-cycle sensitivity (H4)\n- For each `(anchor, off_cycle)` adjacent pair (e.g., 96 vs 48, 672 vs 2016), report the per-period-cell sign of `Δ_med`. H4 is supported iff ≥ 50 % of the adjacent pairs show `|relative Δ| ≤ 0.10` on at least one horizon.\n\n### F.5 Calendar alignment (H2)\n- For each calendar anchor, compare against the geometric mean of its two nearest off-cycle neighbours. H2 is supported iff the anchor's mean MSE is strictly **lower** than that reference on at least 3 of the 4 horizons.\n\n### F.6 No test-set tuning (enforced)\n- The test set is read only in §F.1–F.5 reporting; no threshold, no early-stopping, no model selection touches test data. A separate per-cell log file records every time the test dataset is opened.\n\n### F.7 Visual outputs (described, not produced)\n- A 2-D heatmap of test MSE with rows = `period` (log-scale), columns = `pred_len`, one panel per `(channel_set, seed)`. Cells with violated controls are masked.\n- A line plot of per-horizon mean MSE vs `pred_len`, one curve per `period`.\n\n---\n\n## (G) Feasibility and Runtime Estimate\n\n**Environment (from inspection):** Python 3.12.3; 24 CPU threads; **no GPU** (`nvidia-smi` not available); **no preinstalled PyTorch / NumPy / pandas** (the visible base packages do not include them); system-network-disabled per the task brief; 24 cores reported by `nproc`.\n\n**Dependency chain required at runtime (not preinstalled, to be installed by the runner):**\n\n- `torch` (CPU build), `numpy`, `pandas`, `scikit-learn` (for `StandardScaler`), `tqdm` (optional).\n- PDF-model implementation (workspace is silent on it; installation method **unknown (U1)**). The design does not assume a particular source.\n- ETTm1 CSV (workspace does not contain it; acquisition method **unknown (U2)**).\n\n**Compute estimate (α, β kept symbolic; no fabricated numbers):**\n\n- Let `T` = wall-clock of one `(period, pred_len=96, seed)` cell with the smallest `pred_len` on the 12-month train slice, multivariate, on 24 CPU threads.\n- Empirical observation in the literature (linear-class models) suggests T lies in the **minutes-per-cell** range without GPU; magnetic-cell architectures can be an order of magnitude slower. We therefore bracket as `T_coarse ≈ minutes` and `T_fine ≈ multiple of T_coarse`.\n- Upper-bound cell budget from §C.2 is **116 period-cells**.\n- Sequential-only upper bound: `116 · T` ≈ a single day for T≈10 min/cell; a few days for transformer-class PDF at T≈30 min/cell.\n- Parallel upper bound (cell-level independence with seed and period): 24-cell parallelism, i.e. **up to ~24× speed-up** from the 24 threads.\n\n**Intended runtime posture** (the design constraint, not a measured number):\n\n- *With a linear/moving-average-class PDF:* full sweep feasible in **under one day of CPU time**, single machine.\n- *With a transformer-class PDF on CPU:* the full sweep is at the edge of the actor step budget; the recommended posture is to **drop the secondary `seq_len=336` runs** and run **a 7-value coarse sweep only**, deferring the fine sweep to a separate run.\n\n**Feasibility levers** (any of which are acceptable variance-reducing moves if runtime pressure arises):\n\n1. Drop secondary `seq_len=336`.\n2. Reduce off-cycle values from 5 to 3.\n3. Take a stratified subset of test windows instead of all test windows (logged, not used for selection).\n4. Promote the fine sweep to 2 seeds instead of 3 if the secondary `seq_len` view is kept.\n\nThe design **explicitly does not** recommend dropping calendar anchors; these carry the H2 claim.\n\n**Reproducibility posture:** pinned `pip` lock-file at the start of execution; SHA256 of the CSV; serialised scaler; serialised seeds; per-cell log including commit hash of the PDF implementation if available.\n\n---\n\n## (H) Explicit Limitations\n\nThe design has the following limitations, tied directly to the unknowns in the approved plan:\n\n1. **Implementation-agnostic (U1).** The workspace provides no PDF-model code. The design remains valid across the family of seasonality-aware forecasters that consume an integer period hyperparameter, but **cannot be claimed** to characterise any specific named model until U1 is resolved. When U1 is resolved, the metadata fields in §B should be filled in and the controls in §E re-inspected.\n2. **Knob interpretation (U5) unknown.** It is not known whether the period knob touches only the input reshape or also internal loss/regulariser terms. Some period values may be illegal in some implementations; these cells will be marked `illegal_period` rather than evaluated.\n3. **Auxiliary knobs (U6) unknown.** If the model exposes additional period-adjacent knobs (multi-period, learnable period, period dropout), the design assumes they are pinned or disabled. If the implementation prevents this, the affected cells are excluded from cross-implementation claims.\n4. **Seed budget for inference is intentionally small.** With n=3 fine seeds and n=1 coarse seed, the hypothesis test in §F.2 reports **direction, not p-value**. This is by design.\n5. **No-GPU constraint (A6).** Without a GPU, transformer-class period cells may force the §G fallback \"coarse-only\" plan. The design absorbs this by separating the coarse and fine sweeps.\n6. **Dataset provenance (A6 and U2).** The CSV is not in the workspace. The design depends on it being obtained via whatever acquisition method the runtime provides; if the version differs from the canonical 12/4/4 split's reference CSV, splits are *re-mapped* and the SHA256 logged.\n7. **Channel-set ambiguity (U4).** If the implementation only supports univariate forecasting on `OT`, the multivariate cells are abandoned (logged, not renormalised away). The hypotheses remain evaluable on the univariate case.\n8. **Calendar anchoring is empirical, not theoretical.** H2's claim that calendar anchors outperform off-cycle values is an empirical prior derived from the ETTm1 sampling calendar; it is **not** derived from any specific PDF-model property.\n\n---\n\n## (I) Open Questions Retained for the Researcher\n\nThe following are the same unknowns surfaced in the approved plan; they should be resolved **before** execution begins, but **none of them invalidate** a design-only deliverable.\n\n- **(U1)** Which specific \"PDF model\" reference implementation is intended? No source artefact exists in the workspace, and no widely-cited forecasting model bearing that acronym with a \"period\" knob is identifiable from in-workspace evidence. Possibilities to rule in/out: a specific recent paper's acronym; an internal/proprietary model; a notational variant.\n- **(U2)** Where is the ETTm1 CSV sourced from at runtime, and what is its expected SHA256?\n- **(U3)** What is the training budget (max epochs, early-stopping patience, learning rate schedule) and which non-period hyperparameters are explicitly pinned?\n- **(U4)** Is univariate (`OT` only) or multivariate (7-channel) the canonical evaluation mode for this PDF model on ETTm1, or both?\n- **(U5)** Does the period knob touch only input reshape, or also internal regulariser/loss terms? Which period values are legal under the chosen implementation?\n- **(U6)** Does the implementation expose period-adjacent knobs (multi-period, learnable period, period dropout) that must be pinned or disabled to make the period axis the only varying input?\n\n---\n\n## Appendix — Self-containment Checklist (mirrors the approved plan, restated for traceability)\n\n- Workspace contains exactly one file, `/workspace/task.json`, plus an absent `track.md`. **No PDF-model source, no ETTm1 data, no experiment scaffolding** exists in `/workspace`, `/home/actor`, or `/tmp`. The confirmed `libqpdf.so` and `/bin/pdf*` artefacts are unrelated system files.\n- The plan fixes: (i) `period` as a scalar integer in steps; (ii) calendar anchors at 96/288/672/1440/2880; (iii) off-cycle values at 24/48/192/2016/4320 (upper-capped); (iv) 12/4/4 chronological splits materialised once; (v) `seq_len=96` with optional secondary `seq_len=336`; (vi) `pred_len ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}`; (vii) MSE + MAE on de-normalized targets; (viii) 1 seed for coarse, 3 seeds for fine; (ix) paired-difference comparison; (x) mean ± std aggregation; (xi) no test-set tuning; (xii) implementation-agnostic metadata capture for U1–U6.\n\n**No experimental run is claimed. No metric numbers are reported. No expected outcomes are stated.**\n", "evidence": "\nEvidence (binary inspection records):\n\n1. `/workspace/task.json` is the sole file in `/workspace` and explicitly states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" — supports the design-only mandate and the absence of source material.\n2. `/home/actor` and `/tmp` are empty directories — supports \"no prior experiment scripts in workspace\".\n3. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'ettm1*'` returns no matches — supports \"no ETTm1 CSV in workspace\".\n4. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'patchtst*' / dlinear* / timesnet* / informer* / autoformer*` produces no matches — supports \"no reference implementation in workspace\".\n5. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*.pdf'` returns only `/usr/share/cups/data/form_*.pdf` and similar CUPS printer test pages — confirms there are no research PDFs of the PDF model in the workspace; the `libqpdf.so` and CLI helpers are unrelated.\n6. `python3 --version = 3.12.3`; `pip3 list` head shows only base system packages (no torch/numpy/pandas); `nvidia-smi` is not runnable; `nproc = 24` — supports (G) feasibility narrative and (A6) reproducibility assumption.\n7. ETTm1 calendar facts (15-minute sampling, 96 steps/day, 672 steps/week, 12/4/4-month standard splits) are public reference constants used to anchor (C) and (D); they are not invented experiment statistics.\n8. All metric values, ranges, hypotheses' outcomes, and runtime estimates in the document are described symbolically or as ranges; no specific MSE / MAE numbers are reported.\n\nNo external network access was used, no source repo was cloned, no training was executed. The document is the design deliverable itself.\n", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-3388d572-986d-45b2-878c-c9c46cacc30f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-3388d572-986d-45b2-878c-c9c46cacc30f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final experiment-design document for PDF+ETTm1\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Experiment Design: Effect of the `period` setting on the PDF model \\u2014 ETTm1\\n\\n> **Status:** Design only. Not executed. No result numbers invented. No reference repo adopted. Where the source material is silent, items are flagged **assumed** vs **unknown** using the prefixes **(Ax)** / **(Ux)** from the approved plan.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (A) Research Question and Pre-registered Hypotheses\\n\\n**RQ.** *How do different settings of the `period` hyperparameter of the PDF model affect its forecasting performance on the ETTm1 dataset under a fixed evaluation contract?*\\n\\nThe question is decomposed into four pre-registered claims, all stated in a **two-sided** form so that any observed direction is informative.\\n\\n- **H1 (coarse shape).** Test MSE/MAE vary non-monotonically with `period` across at least two calendar anchors of ETTm1 (daily `96`, weekly `672`).\\n  - *Why it matters.* If the surface were monotonic, then a single extreme value would dominate and the period knob would be effectively redundant given a long enough look-back. Non-monotonicity is the signal that the knob carries real information about cycle-length alignment.\\n- **H2 (alignment prior).** Calendar-anchored periods (multiples of 96 steps, the native daily cycle of ETTm1's 15-minute grid) yield lower test MSE/MAE than the immediately adjacent off-cycle values, on average across the horizon suite.\\n  - *Why it matters.* Separates \\\"period matters\\\" from \\\"period matters only when its value aligns with the data calendar.\\\"\\n- **H3 (horizon dependence).** The magnitude of any `period` effect grows with the forecast horizon `pred_len`, holding look-back fixed.\\n  - *Why it matters.* A purely short-horizon effect (\\u226496 steps) would invalidate the knob for long-range forecasting on ETTm1.\\n- **H4 (off-cycle sensitivity).** Off-cycle values (e.g., `48`, `288`, `1440`, `2016`) still produce comparable or improved performance within a paired-seed difference of at most 10 % MSE on at least one horizon, when measured against the best calendar anchor.\\n  - *Why it matters.* Tests whether the knob is fragile to small mismatches or whether it generalises.\\n\\n**Pre-registration rule.** H1\\u2013H4 are evaluated only on **de-normalized test targets** under the fixed evaluation contract in \\u00a7D. The test split is *never* used for any selection; only val is.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (B) Operational Definition of the `period` Knob\\n\\nThe workspace contains **no PDF-model source, no ETTm1 data, and no prior experiment script** (see inspection in the plan stage). The design is therefore **implementation-agnostic** and pins only what travels with the term itself.\\n\\n**(A2, assumed)** `period \\u2208 \\u2115` is a single positive integer denoting a **cycle length in number of time steps** at the model's native sampling frequency. The knob is consumed by whatever stage of the PDF model would in general consume a seasonality length \\u2014 for implementation families that span decomposers (e.g., moving-average-based linear forecasters), reshape-based encoders, or period-matching decoders, this is the single scalar that the model exposes as \\\"the period.\\\" Whether the period also appears in auxiliary places (positional bias, regulariser, multi-period head) is **unknown (U5)** and is investigated via ablations in \\u00a7G.\\n\\n**Specification language.** For every run we record, *as a metadata field but never as a decision input*:\\n\\n| Metadata field | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| `model_class` | string identifier of the PDF implementation used |\\n| `period` | the integer value under test |\\n| `period_consumers` | which callable modules read `period` (e.g., decomposition window, reshape grid) \\u2014 if unknown, log `unknown` |\\n| `period_dependent_init` | are any weight initialisations conditioned on `period`? \\u2014 flag if yes |\\n\\nThis metadata is exactly what lets the conclusions remain valid across implementations when U1 is later resolved.\\n\\n**Working vocabulary used throughout this document:**\\n\\n- \\\"Calendar anchor\\\" = a `period` that is an integer multiple of 96 (the daily cycle of ETTm1 at 15-minute resolution).\\n- \\\"Off-cycle value\\\" = a `period` that is *not* an integer multiple of 96.\\n- \\\"Period cell\\\" = the tuple `(period, pred_len, seed)` that produces a single evaluated run.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (C) Sweep \\u2014 Period Values and Rationale Per Value\\n\\n### C.1 Coarse sweep (1 seed per cell, all horizons)\\nCalendar anchors \\u2014 chosen because they are the empirically plausible seasonal cycles at 15-minute resolution:\\n\\n| `period` | Rationale |\\n|---|---|\\n| **96** | 1 day. ETTm1 is electricity-transformer load with a clear 24-h cycle; this is the **shortest natural anchor** and the most commonly used default in linear-decomposition forecasters. |\\n| **288** | 3 days. Tests sensitivity to a sub-weekly but multi-daily choice. |\\n| **672** | 1 week (96 \\u00d7 7). ETTm1 exhibits weekly structure (weekday/weekend load shift); this is the **most data-aligned weekly anchor**. |\\n| **1440** | 10 days. Off-week but still on the day grid; controls for weekly dominance. |\\n| **2880** | ~30 days. A monthly anchor; tests long-period extrapolation given fixed look-back. |\\n\\nOff-cycle robustness values \\u2014 chosen because they do **not** land on a calendar multiple:\\n\\n| `period` | Rationale |\\n|---|---|\\n| **24** | 6 hours. Far shorter than a day; tests the lower end of the regime. |\\n| **48** | 12 hours. Off-grid; tests whether \\\"near-daily\\\" but unaligned hurts. |\\n| **192** | 2 days. Day-grid but skips the canonical 1-day value. |\\n| **2016** | ~14 days. Off-grid between weekly and bi-weekly. |\\n| **4320** | cap = floor(train_len / 2) under the 12/4/4 split. Stress-test of \\\"very long period\\\". |\\n\\n**Reference baseline** **(A4, assumed)**: the implementation's default `period`. Numeric value **unknown (U4)**, to be clipped into the sweep lattice at logging time.\\n\\n### C.2 Fine sweep (3 seeds per cell)\\nConducted only around the two cells that win the coarse sweep on validation-MSE per horizon:\\n\\n- A neighbourhood of width \\u00b11 step at the winning off-cycle value.\\n- A neighbourhood of width \\u00b124 steps at the winning calendar anchor.\\n- The implementation default (if not already in the coarse sweep) is added with 3 seeds.\\n\\nThis is the **only** step that uses more than one seed, in line with the approved plan.\\n\\n**Sweep cell budget (upper bound, used only for feasibility planning in \\u00a7G):**\\n\\n| Stage | `period` values | horizons | seeds | cells |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Coarse | 11 (5 anchors + 5 off + default) | 4 | 1 | \\u2264 44 |\\n| Fine | \\u2264 6 (the two winners \\u00b1 their neighbourhoods + default) | 4 | 3 | \\u2264 72 |\\n| Total | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | **\\u2264 116 period-cells** |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (D) Dataset and Fixed Evaluation Contract\\n\\n### D.1 Dataset\\n- **Name:** ETTm1. Publicly distributed as `ETTm1.csv` with column `date` + 6 oil-temperature features (`HUFL`, `HULL`, `MUFL`, `MULL`, `LUFL`, `LULL`) + the target `OT`. **(Ax, assumed)** \\u2014 file is not present in the workspace and must be obtained at runtime per environment note.\\n\\n### D.2 Splits (identical across every period cell)\\n- **Chronological 12 / 4 / 4 months**, the standard split used by Autoformer / Informer / PatchTST literature on ETTm1.\\n- Train slice = first 12 months; val = next 4 months; test = final 4 months.\\n- Sliding windows for evaluation: for each `(seq_len, pred_len)`, evaluate on every contiguous window whose *prediction endpoint* lies in the test slice, **and only in the test slice**; this is the rolling \\\"last-window\\\" protocol.\\n- Splits are **materialised once** at the start of the experiment and reused; a hash of `(timestamps, scaler_state)` is logged so reproducibility can be checked.\\n\\n### D.3 Normalization\\n- `StandardScaler` fit on the **train slice only** (mean and std of each channel). Applied identically to train, val, and test. The scaler state is serialised.\\n- Metrics computed on **de-normalized** predictions and targets. **(Ax, assumed)**\\n\\n### D.4 Fixed evaluation contract (every cell must obey)\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| `seq_len` (look-back) | **96** (with **336** as mandatory secondary length if runner budget allows; logged but not used for any decision) |\\n| `pred_len` (horizon suite) | **{96, 192, 336, 720}** |\\n| Channels, multivariate | all 7 (`HUFL`\\u2013`OT`) used as input and forecast jointly |\\n| Channels, univariate (secondary) | `OT` only, reported separately |\\n| Loss during training | MSE on (possibly normalised) targets, model-specific |\\n| Reporting metrics | **MSE** and **MAE**, on de-normalized test targets |\\n| Aggregation across seeds | **mean \\u00b1 std** |\\n| Selection variable | **val MSE**, per `(period, pred_len, seed)` cell |\\n| Test-set usage | **none** for selection, thresholding, or hyper-tuning |\\n\\n### D.5 Seeds protocol\\n- Fixed cross-cell seed list: `S = {s1, s2, s3}` for fine cells; `S = {s1}` for coarse cells.\\n- Each seed initialises: Python `random`, NumPy, PyTorch (CPU), and `torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True`.\\n- Data-loader worker seeds = seed + worker_id (modulo 1e9).\\n- A single seed run is **fully reproducible** under these settings on the same pinned dependency versions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (E) Controls (to isolate the effect of `period`)\\n\\nThe intent is that **any metric difference between two `period` cells of equal seed, horizon, and look-back is attributable solely to `period`**, up to the residual noise acknowledged in (F).\\n\\n| Item | Treatment | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Raw data | single CSV, single SHA256 | eliminates data-drift |\\n| Splits | materialised once, hashed | eliminates split-noise |\\n| Scaler | fit once on train, reused | eliminates normalization bias |\\n| `seq_len` | fixed across the design (with secondary length logged) | isolates the period axis |\\n| `pred_len` | fixed per cell | equal-horizon comparability |\\n| Non-period hyperparameters (e.g., hidden size, depth, LR, batch size, optimizer, dropout, weight decay, gradient clipping) | **identical** across cells | removes optimisation confound |\\n| Training budget | fixed max-epoch budget + identical early-stopping rule on val MSE per cell | removes training-time drift |\\n| Multi-period / learnable-period / period-dropout knobs **(U6)** | **pinned** to a constant value or disabled; if disabling breaks the model, that is documented and the cell is excluded | eliminates auxiliary knobs |\\n| Hardware and software | pinned versions; CPU-only | removes hardware variance |\\n| Seeds | cross-cell identical seed list | enables paired-difference in \\u00a7F |\\n| Selection variable | val MSE only | no test-set tuning |\\n\\nIf any control above must be violated because the implementation requires it (e.g., the period knob forces a specific input shape), the violation is logged as `control_violation` metadata and the affected cells are not used for cross-implementation claims.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (F) Analysis Plan\\n\\n### F.1 Aggregation\\n- For each `(period, pred_len, channel_set)` cell with multiple seeds, compute mean and std of test MSE and test MAE across seeds.\\n- A grand-mean table over `pred_len` for each `period` is reported as a secondary summary.\\n\\n### F.2 Paired-difference comparison (the primary signal)\\n- For any two period values `p` and `q`, form `\\u0394_i = metric(p, seed_i) \\u2212 metric(q, seed_i)` within the **same seed `i`** at the **same horizon and the same channel set**.\\n- Hypothesis tests:\\n  - **Wilcoxon signed-rank** across seed pairs (robust to non-Gaussianity, 3-seed-friendly).\\n  - Sign-test over seeds as a non-parametric fallback if n=3 is too small for Wilcoxon power (n=3 yields no p-value; only direction is reported).\\n- Effect size: **median paired difference** and **relative paired difference** (`median(\\u0394) / median(metric(q))`).\\n- A confidence band is **not** reported at n=3; the design specifies \\\"n seeds for variance read\\\", not \\\"power analysis\\\", per the approved plan.\\n\\n### F.3 Per-horizon breakdown (H3)\\n- Estimate the effect size `|median(\\u0394_bad \\u2212 \\u0394_good)|` per horizon and report its sign. The H3 claim is supported if the magnitude grows monotonically (or at least non-trivially) from `pred_len=96` to `pred_len=720`.\\n\\n### F.4 Off-cycle sensitivity (H4)\\n- For each `(anchor, off_cycle)` adjacent pair (e.g., 96 vs 48, 672 vs 2016), report the per-period-cell sign of `\\u0394_med`. H4 is supported iff \\u2265 50 % of the adjacent pairs show `|relative \\u0394| \\u2264 0.10` on at least one horizon.\\n\\n### F.5 Calendar alignment (H2)\\n- For each calendar anchor, compare against the geometric mean of its two nearest off-cycle neighbours. H2 is supported iff the anchor's mean MSE is strictly **lower** than that reference on at least 3 of the 4 horizons.\\n\\n### F.6 No test-set tuning (enforced)\\n- The test set is read only in \\u00a7F.1\\u2013F.5 reporting; no threshold, no early-stopping, no model selection touches test data. A separate per-cell log file records every time the test dataset is opened.\\n\\n### F.7 Visual outputs (described, not produced)\\n- A 2-D heatmap of test MSE with rows = `period` (log-scale), columns = `pred_len`, one panel per `(channel_set, seed)`. Cells with violated controls are masked.\\n- A line plot of per-horizon mean MSE vs `pred_len`, one curve per `period`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (G) Feasibility and Runtime Estimate\\n\\n**Environment (from inspection):** Python 3.12.3; 24 CPU threads; **no GPU** (`nvidia-smi` not available); **no preinstalled PyTorch / NumPy / pandas** (the visible base packages do not include them); system-network-disabled per the task brief; 24 cores reported by `nproc`.\\n\\n**Dependency chain required at runtime (not preinstalled, to be installed by the runner):**\\n\\n- `torch` (CPU build), `numpy`, `pandas`, `scikit-learn` (for `StandardScaler`), `tqdm` (optional).\\n- PDF-model implementation (workspace is silent on it; installation method **unknown (U1)**). The design does not assume a particular source.\\n- ETTm1 CSV (workspace does not contain it; acquisition method **unknown (U2)**).\\n\\n**Compute estimate (\\u03b1, \\u03b2 kept symbolic; no fabricated numbers):**\\n\\n- Let `T` = wall-clock of one `(period, pred_len=96, seed)` cell with the smallest `pred_len` on the 12-month train slice, multivariate, on 24 CPU threads.\\n- Empirical observation in the literature (linear-class models) suggests T lies in the **minutes-per-cell** range without GPU; magnetic-cell architectures can be an order of magnitude slower. We therefore bracket as `T_coarse \\u2248 minutes` and `T_fine \\u2248 multiple of T_coarse`.\\n- Upper-bound cell budget from \\u00a7C.2 is **116 period-cells**.\\n- Sequential-only upper bound: `116 \\u00b7 T` \\u2248 a single day for T\\u224810 min/cell; a few days for transformer-class PDF at T\\u224830 min/cell.\\n- Parallel upper bound (cell-level independence with seed and period): 24-cell parallelism, i.e. **up to ~24\\u00d7 speed-up** from the 24 threads.\\n\\n**Intended runtime posture** (the design constraint, not a measured number):\\n\\n- *With a linear/moving-average-class PDF:* full sweep feasible in **under one day of CPU time**, single machine.\\n- *With a transformer-class PDF on CPU:* the full sweep is at the edge of the actor step budget; the recommended posture is to **drop the secondary `seq_len=336` runs** and run **a 7-value coarse sweep only**, deferring the fine sweep to a separate run.\\n\\n**Feasibility levers** (any of which are acceptable variance-reducing moves if runtime pressure arises):\\n\\n1. Drop secondary `seq_len=336`.\\n2. Reduce off-cycle values from 5 to 3.\\n3. Take a stratified subset of test windows instead of all test windows (logged, not used for selection).\\n4. Promote the fine sweep to 2 seeds instead of 3 if the secondary `seq_len` view is kept.\\n\\nThe design **explicitly does not** recommend dropping calendar anchors; these carry the H2 claim.\\n\\n**Reproducibility posture:** pinned `pip` lock-file at the start of execution; SHA256 of the CSV; serialised scaler; serialised seeds; per-cell log including commit hash of the PDF implementation if available.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (H) Explicit Limitations\\n\\nThe design has the following limitations, tied directly to the unknowns in the approved plan:\\n\\n1. **Implementation-agnostic (U1).** The workspace provides no PDF-model code. The design remains valid across the family of seasonality-aware forecasters that consume an integer period hyperparameter, but **cannot be claimed** to characterise any specific named model until U1 is resolved. When U1 is resolved, the metadata fields in \\u00a7B should be filled in and the controls in \\u00a7E re-inspected.\\n2. **Knob interpretation (U5) unknown.** It is not known whether the period knob touches only the input reshape or also internal loss/regulariser terms. Some period values may be illegal in some implementations; these cells will be marked `illegal_period` rather than evaluated.\\n3. **Auxiliary knobs (U6) unknown.** If the model exposes additional period-adjacent knobs (multi-period, learnable period, period dropout), the design assumes they are pinned or disabled. If the implementation prevents this, the affected cells are excluded from cross-implementation claims.\\n4. **Seed budget for inference is intentionally small.** With n=3 fine seeds and n=1 coarse seed, the hypothesis test in \\u00a7F.2 reports **direction, not p-value**. This is by design.\\n5. **No-GPU constraint (A6).** Without a GPU, transformer-class period cells may force the \\u00a7G fallback \\\"coarse-only\\\" plan. The design absorbs this by separating the coarse and fine sweeps.\\n6. **Dataset provenance (A6 and U2).** The CSV is not in the workspace. The design depends on it being obtained via whatever acquisition method the runtime provides; if the version differs from the canonical 12/4/4 split's reference CSV, splits are *re-mapped* and the SHA256 logged.\\n7. **Channel-set ambiguity (U4).** If the implementation only supports univariate forecasting on `OT`, the multivariate cells are abandoned (logged, not renormalised away). The hypotheses remain evaluable on the univariate case.\\n8. **Calendar anchoring is empirical, not theoretical.** H2's claim that calendar anchors outperform off-cycle values is an empirical prior derived from the ETTm1 sampling calendar; it is **not** derived from any specific PDF-model property.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (I) Open Questions Retained for the Researcher\\n\\nThe following are the same unknowns surfaced in the approved plan; they should be resolved **before** execution begins, but **none of them invalidate** a design-only deliverable.\\n\\n- **(U1)** Which specific \\\"PDF model\\\" reference implementation is intended? No source artefact exists in the workspace, and no widely-cited forecasting model bearing that acronym with a \\\"period\\\" knob is identifiable from in-workspace evidence. Possibilities to rule in/out: a specific recent paper's acronym; an internal/proprietary model; a notational variant.\\n- **(U2)** Where is the ETTm1 CSV sourced from at runtime, and what is its expected SHA256?\\n- **(U3)** What is the training budget (max epochs, early-stopping patience, learning rate schedule) and which non-period hyperparameters are explicitly pinned?\\n- **(U4)** Is univariate (`OT` only) or multivariate (7-channel) the canonical evaluation mode for this PDF model on ETTm1, or both?\\n- **(U5)** Does the period knob touch only input reshape, or also internal regulariser/loss terms? Which period values are legal under the chosen implementation?\\n- **(U6)** Does the implementation expose period-adjacent knobs (multi-period, learnable period, period dropout) that must be pinned or disabled to make the period axis the only varying input?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix \\u2014 Self-containment Checklist (mirrors the approved plan, restated for traceability)\\n\\n- Workspace contains exactly one file, `/workspace/task.json`, plus an absent `track.md`. **No PDF-model source, no ETTm1 data, no experiment scaffolding** exists in `/workspace`, `/home/actor`, or `/tmp`. The confirmed `libqpdf.so` and `/bin/pdf*` artefacts are unrelated system files.\\n- The plan fixes: (i) `period` as a scalar integer in steps; (ii) calendar anchors at 96/288/672/1440/2880; (iii) off-cycle values at 24/48/192/2016/4320 (upper-capped); (iv) 12/4/4 chronological splits materialised once; (v) `seq_len=96` with optional secondary `seq_len=336`; (vi) `pred_len \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}`; (vii) MSE + MAE on de-normalized targets; (viii) 1 seed for coarse, 3 seeds for fine; (ix) paired-difference comparison; (x) mean \\u00b1 std aggregation; (xi) no test-set tuning; (xii) implementation-agnostic metadata capture for U1\\u2013U6.\\n\\n**No experimental run is claimed. No metric numbers are reported. No expected outcomes are stated.**\\n\", \"evidence\": \"\\nEvidence (binary inspection records):\\n\\n1. `/workspace/task.json` is the sole file in `/workspace` and explicitly states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" \\u2014 supports the design-only mandate and the absence of source material.\\n2. `/home/actor` and `/tmp` are empty directories \\u2014 supports \\\"no prior experiment scripts in workspace\\\".\\n3. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'ettm1*'` returns no matches \\u2014 supports \\\"no ETTm1 CSV in workspace\\\".\\n4. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'patchtst*' / dlinear* / timesnet* / informer* / autoformer*` produces no matches \\u2014 supports \\\"no reference implementation in workspace\\\".\\n5. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*.pdf'` returns only `/usr/share/cups/data/form_*.pdf` and similar CUPS printer test pages \\u2014 confirms there are no research PDFs of the PDF model in the workspace; the `libqpdf.so` and CLI helpers are unrelated.\\n6. `python3 --version = 3.12.3`; `pip3 list` head shows only base system packages (no torch/numpy/pandas); `nvidia-smi` is not runnable; `nproc = 24` \\u2014 supports (G) feasibility narrative and (A6) reproducibility assumption.\\n7. ETTm1 calendar facts (15-minute sampling, 96 steps/day, 672 steps/week, 12/4/4-month standard splits) are public reference constants used to anchor (C) and (D); they are not invented experiment statistics.\\n8. All metric values, ranges, hypotheses' outcomes, and runtime estimates in the document are described symbolically or as ranges; no specific MSE / MAE numbers are reported.\\n\\nNo external network access was used, no source repo was cloned, no training was executed. The document is the design deliverable itself.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bf775b03-b340-4130-afad-2642069088e7", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
